  "llm_training.model.gpt2.model.TransformerBlock": {
   "name": "TransformerBlock",
   "path": "src/llm_training/model/gpt2/model.py",
   "line": 14,
   "header": [
    "# Copyright (c) Sebastian Raschka under Apache License 2.0 (see LICENSES/LLMs-from-scratch.txt).",
    "# Source: https://github.com/rasbt/LLMs-from-scratch",
    "# Modified: selected symbols, reorganized imports/modules and formatting for llm-training.",
    "# Also modified: optional GPT-2 init (init.py) and tied output head; defaults keep source behavior."
   ],
   "forward": {
    "line": 31,
    "code": "def forward(self, x):\n    # Shortcut connection for attention block\n    shortcut = x\n    x = self.norm1(x)\n    x = self.att(x)  # Shape [batch_size, num_tokens, emb_size]\n    x = self.drop_resid(x)\n    x = x + shortcut  # Add the original input back\n\n    # Shortcut connection for feed-forward block\n    shortcut = x\n    x = self.norm2(x)\n    x = self.ff(x)\n    x = self.drop_resid(x)\n    x = x + shortcut  # Add the original input back\n\n    return x\n"
   },
   "note": {
    "title": "GPT-2 transformer block",
    "text": "Two sublayers, each pre-normalized and wrapped in a residual connection: `x = x + drop(att(norm1(x)))`, then `x = x + drop(ff(norm2(x)))`. The additions aren't modules, so they don't appear as boxes; they're the `x + shortcut` lines in the code below.\n\nThe residual stream keeps the same width from the first block to the last. Each sublayer reads a normalized copy and writes back an update.",
    "see": [
     "docs/gpt2.md#attention-and-block-details"
    ]
   }
  },
  "llm_training.model.gpt2.layers.LayerNorm": {
   "name": "LayerNorm",
   "path": "src/llm_training/model/gpt2/layers.py",
   "line": 10,
   "header": [
    "# Copyright (c) Sebastian Raschka under Apache License 2.0 (see LICENSES/LLMs-from-scratch.txt).",
    "# Source: https://github.com/rasbt/LLMs-from-scratch",
    "# Modified: selected symbols, reorganized imports/modules and formatting for llm-training.",
    "# Also modified: GELU's float32 constant is a non-persistent buffer instead of rebuilt per call."
   ],
   "forward": {
    "line": 17,
    "code": "def forward(self, x):\n    mean = x.mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n    var = x.var(dim=-1, keepdim=True, unbiased=False)\n    norm_x = (x - mean) / torch.sqrt(var + self.eps)\n    return self.scale * norm_x + self.shift\n"
   },
   "note": {
    "title": "LayerNorm",
    "text": "For each token, subtracts the mean across the embedding dimension, divides by the standard deviation (with eps 1e-5), then applies a learned per-channel `scale` and `shift`. Two vectors of width `emb_dim`."
   }
  },
  "llm_training.model.gpt2.attention.MultiHeadAttention": {
   "name": "MultiHeadAttention",
   "path": "src/llm_training/model/gpt2/attention.py",
   "line": 10,
   "header": [
    "# Copyright (c) Sebastian Raschka under Apache License 2.0 (see LICENSES/LLMs-from-scratch.txt).",
    "# Source: https://github.com/rasbt/LLMs-from-scratch",
    "# Modified: selected symbols, reorganized imports/modules and formatting for llm-training.",
    "# Also modified: optional fused SDPA path (attn_impl=\"sdpa\"); the default is the source code."
   ],
   "forward": {
    "line": 31,
    "code": "def forward(self, x):\n    b, num_tokens, d_in = x.shape\n\n    keys = self.W_key(x)  # Shape: (b, num_tokens, d_out)\n    queries = self.W_query(x)\n    values = self.W_value(x)\n\n    # We implicitly split the matrix by adding a `num_heads` dimension\n    # Unroll last dim: (b, num_tokens, d_out) -> (b, num_tokens, num_heads, head_dim)\n    keys = keys.view(b, num_tokens, self.num_heads, self.head_dim)\n    values = values.view(b, num_tokens, self.num_heads, self.head_dim)\n    queries = queries.view(b, num_tokens, self.num_heads, self.head_dim)\n\n    # Transpose: (b, num_tokens, num_heads, head_dim) -> (b, num_heads, num_tokens, head_dim)\n    keys = keys.transpose(1, 2)\n    queries = queries.transpose(1, 2)\n    values = values.transpose(1, 2)\n\n    if self.attn_impl == \"sdpa\":\n        # Fused kernel: never materializes the (T, T) score matrix. Dropout draws\n        # differ from the explicit path, so the two only match with dropout off.\n        context_vec = torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention(\n            queries,\n            keys,\n            values,\n            dropout_p=self.dropout.p if self.training else 0.0,\n            is_causal=True,\n        ).transpose(1, 2)\n    else:\n        # Compute scaled dot-product attention (aka self-attention) with a causal mask\n        attn_scores = queries @ keys.transpose(2, 3)  # Dot product for each head\n\n        # Original mask truncated to the number of tokens and converted to boolean\n        mask_bool = self.mask.bool()[:num_tokens, :num_tokens]\n\n        # Use the mask to fill attention scores\n        attn_scores.masked_fill_(mask_bool, -torch.inf)\n\n        attn_weights = torch.softmax(attn_scores / keys.shape[-1] ** 0.5, dim=-1)\n        attn_weights = self.dropout(attn_weights)\n\n        # Shape: (b, num_tokens, num_heads, head_dim)\n        context_vec = (attn_weights @ values).transpose(1, 2)\n\n    # Combine heads, where self.d_out = self.num_heads * self.head_dim\n    context_vec = context_vec.reshape(b, num_tokens, self.d_out)\n    context_vec = self.out_proj(context_vec)  # optional projection\n\n    return context_vec\n"
   },
   "note": {
    "title": "Causal multi-head attention",
    "text": "Queries, keys and values come from three separate linear maps. Each is split into `n_heads` heads of width `emb_dim / n_heads`. Every head scores each query against every earlier key with `Q Kᵀ / √head_dim`; a causal mask sets later positions to −∞ before the softmax. The weighted sum of values goes back through `out_proj`.\n\nThe split into heads is a reshape and a transpose (`view`, `transpose`), not a module, so it shows in the code rather than as a box. With `attn_impl: sdpa`, PyTorch's fused kernel does the scoring without materializing the T × T matrix; the explicit path builds it and applies dropout to it.\n\nThe `mask` buffer is `context_length × context_length` and persistent, so it is saved in every checkpoint. Making it non-persistent is backlog item A7.",
    "see": [
     "docs/gpt2.md#attention-and-block-details"
    ]
   }
  },
